/-!
# Intersection multiplicity decreases after blowing up a regular curve point

Source: Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Tag 0BI7 and the preceding
`equation-multiplicity` (blueprint: lem-per-surface-curve-intersection-blowup-decrease).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory TopologicalSpace

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

/-- The local intersection multiplicity of the closed subschemes defined by `I` and `J`.
The sum ideal defines their scheme-theoretic intersection. Its stalk ideal is the supremum
of the images of its section ideals on all affine neighbourhoods of `x`; the quotient is
therefore the intersection's local ring, as a module over the ambient local ring.
This definition also covers infinite length and points outside the intersection.

Source: Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, equation-multiplicity preceding Tag 0BI7
(blueprint: lem-per-surface-curve-intersection-blowup-decrease). -/
noncomputable def localIntersectionMultiplicity {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (I J : X.IdealSheafData) (x : X) : ℕ∞ :=
  Module.length (X.presheaf.stalk x)
    ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ⧸
      ⨆ (U : X.affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ U.1),
        Ideal.map (X.presheaf.germ U.1 x hx).hom ((I ⊔ J).ideal U))

/-- Blowing up a closed point where an integral curve is regular preserves that curve,
meets it once with exceptional multiplicity one, and strictly lowers its intersection
multiplicity with any closed subscheme missing its generic point.

`p` is represented on the closed subscheme `Y`; its image in `X` is the closed centre.
The parameter `C` is constrained to be exactly the vanishing ideal of that reduced point,
so the blowup and exceptional ideal are the canonical ones. The strict transforms are
represented by the kernels of their canonical closed immersions into `C.blowup`.

Source: Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Tag 0BI7, with equation-multiplicity
(blueprint: lem-per-surface-curve-intersection-blowup-decrease).

Proof plan, following all eight steps of the node:
1. The closed immersion gives a surjection of stalks. The integral curve's stalk at its
   closed point has dimension one. Regularity implies normality by
   lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-dim-one-iff-normal, and lem-sc-normal-dim1-dvr gives a DVR.
2. Lift a uniformizer to the ambient local ring. On a sufficiently small affine open,
   the point ideal is the sum of the curve ideal and this parameter. Its restriction
   to the curve is an invertible ideal (def-nagata-invertible-ideal).
3. By thm-nagata-strict-transform-is-blowup the curve's strict transform is its blowup
   in that ideal. Apply cor-nagata-blowup-cartier-iso. The exceptional intersection is
   the inverse image of the reduced point, hence a unique point q; use
   thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center for the exceptional Cartier ideal.
4. The other subscheme's ideal in the DVR is nonzero and proper, hence generated by
   a nonzero element a divisible by the uniformizer. The principal quotient has finite
   length by lem-weil-pbc-dim-le-one-principal-quotient-finite-length, and positive
   length since it is nonzero. Surjectivity identifies ambient and curve submodules.
5. Use the uniformizer chart of prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart and
   def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring; prop-nagata-global-blowup-cover-independent identifies
   this with the global blowup over the affine open. Send y/t^n to its quotient in
   the curve ring. Cancellation in that domain makes the map well defined and gives
   the inverse of the strict-transform isomorphism, by scheme-theoretic closure.
6. By lemma-nagata-extended-ideal-affine-chart and
   lemma-nagata-chart-extended-ideal-principal, the exceptional ideal is generated by
   t on the chart. Its intersection stalk is the residue field of the curve DVR,
   and consequently has length one over the ambient stalk.
7. Choose f in the other subscheme ideal mapping to a generator a. The element f/t
   lies in its strict-transform ideal, by the localization-kernel description of
   def-nagata-strict-transform and def-nagata-scheme-theoretic-image. Thus its image
   ideal K in the DVR contains b = a/t; if q belongs to the strict transform, K is proper.
8. Multiplication by t identifies R/(b) with the proper submodule tR/(a) of R/(a).
   Pull back strict submodule chains along R/(b) surjecting onto R/K and append R/(a).
   This increases the chain length by one, proving the strict decrease for
   def-weil-pbc-module-length. For original length one the conditional case is empty. -/
theorem curve_intersection_blowup_decrease
    {X : Scheme.{u}} [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    (Y Z : X.IdealSheafData) [IsIntegral Y.subscheme]
    (hDim : topologicalKrullDim Y.subscheme = 1)
    (p : Y.subscheme) (hp : IsClosed ({Y.subschemeι p} : Set X))
    (hpZ : Y.subschemeι p ∈ Z.support)
    [IsRegularLocalRing (Y.subscheme.presheaf.stalk p)]
    (η : Y.subscheme) (hη : IsGenericPoint η Set.univ)
    (hηZ : Y.subschemeι η ∉ Z.support)
    (C : X.IdealSheafData) (hC : C = vanishingIdeal ⟨{Y.subschemeι p}, hp⟩) :
    0 < localIntersectionMultiplicity Y Z (Y.subschemeι p) ∧
    localIntersectionMultiplicity Y Z (Y.subschemeι p) < ⊤ ∧
    IsIso (strictTransformToSource Y.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl) ∧
    ∃ q : C.blowup,
      ((strictTransformMap Y.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl).ker.support :
          Set C.blowup) ∩
        ((C.comap C.blowupMap).support : Set C.blowup) = {q} ∧
      localIntersectionMultiplicity
        (strictTransformMap Y.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl).ker
        (C.comap C.blowupMap) q = 1 ∧
      (q ∈ (strictTransformMap Z.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl).ker.support →
        localIntersectionMultiplicity
          (strictTransformMap Y.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl).ker
          (strictTransformMap Z.subschemeι C.blowupMap C.support.compl).ker q <
            localIntersectionMultiplicity Y Z (Y.subschemeι p)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData
